alex simon is the man behind  zero to epic a movement to promote  electric scooters and bikes as an  alternative to cars for short trips  why does it take you know an suv to move  a person for a mile or two  so micro micromobiles provide the  solution  to raise awareness next month simon will  embark on a 30-day  3 thousand plus mile journey from boston  to the oregon coast  all on his electric scooter i'll be  riding about 150 miles a day on average  give or take and that's about eight  hours a day on a scooter  over time simon says many will have to  use electric transportation out of  necessity  there are more people moving into cities  so in the next decade or so you're going  to see a lot more traffic  a lot more congestion a lot more  pollution a lot more noise  some local businesses are noticing this  trend already  here at southwest bikes clean commuting  is growing in popularity with three of  these e-bikes being sold in the last  week alone when we first started doing  it we weren't sure if it was going to  be popular or not but it's really  exceeded our expectations  rocky height is the owner of southwest  bikes near 95 and centennial parkway in  the northwest valley  he thinks e-bikes will be the fastest  growing segment of his store  e-commuter bikes are not that expensive  and you can put racks and haul anything  you want on them nowadays  both height and simon agree the next  step is for more cities to provide  infrastructure so people can e-bike  skate and scoot safely kate houston 8  news now
 

